  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Near-critical to critical fire weather this afternoon and\n  evening from hot, dry and breezy conditions. \n\n- Wildfire smoke will continue to impact the region.\n\nA large ridge of high pressure will maintain hot, dry conditions \nacross western Montana and north-central Idaho into early next \nweek. An upper level wave seen in GOES Water Vapor imagery\napproaching the Pacific Northwest coast this morning is projected\nto flatten the ridge by this afternoon and evening. As the wave\npasses, mid-level winds will increase, yielding stronger afternoon\nwind gusts. The combination of heat, single-digit to low- double-\ndigit relative humidity, gusty winds, and receptive fuels will \ndrive critical fire weather conditions. Red Flag Warnings remain \nin effect, with peak concerns focused across southwest and west- \ncentral Montana, as well as the Palouse and Hells Canyon regions.\n\nWildfire smoke remains the primary forecast challenge. Wildfires\nremained active upstream in Oregon and Washington overnight, with\nplumes of smoke extending downstream into the Northern Rockies. \nWidespread smoke will delay valley inversion breaks, with dense \nsmoke remaining trapped through the morning hours. Notably, \npersistent smoke cover may attenuate solar radiation and suppress \natmospheric mixing into the early afternoon, locally capping \nmaximum temperatures and wind gusts below forecast for some areas.\nNonetheless, the incoming upper level wave will help improve \nvertical mixing, focusing the fire weather threat of hot, dry, \nand windy conditions to the mid-afternoon to early evening\ntimeframe. \n\nSubtle monsoonal moisture advecting from the south will introduce\na low chance of isolated thunderstorms by late afternoon through\nthis evening, mainly across southwest Montana and southern Lemhi \nCounty. Due to an exceptionally dry sub-cloud layer, rainfall will\nlargely evaporate as virga, creating the risk for dry lightning \nignitions and sudden, erratic microburst gusts between 40 and 55 \nmph.\n\nMonday through Next Friday:\n\nConfidence is gradually increasing that mid-level moisture will \nsettle in by mid-to-late next week, sustaining periodic \nthunderstorm chances rather than producing widespread, soaking \nprecipitation. Higher confidence exists for temperatures \nmoderating back toward seasonal normals in the mid-to-upper 80s. A\nmajor weather pattern change, such as a strong, deep storm system\nfrom the Pacific Northwest, is necessary to significantly \nsuppress fire activity and clear the smoke. Current forecasts show\nonly a 20 to 30 percent chance of such a system arriving by the \nmiddle to late portions of next week.",
